This only reads `accum[0..3][0..3]`.

## Step 2 — Semantic Distinguishability

The mutation adds an extra row to the accumulator array (`accum[4][0..3]`), but this extra row is:
1. Never written to (the update loops only go from `i = 0` to `i < 4`, i.e., `i ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3}`)


2. Never read from (the write loops only go from `i = 0` to `i < 4`)

The extra allocated memory remains untouched throughout execution. Since the fifth row is never accessed, the computation produces identical results regardless of whether the array has 4 or 5 rows.

## Step 3 — Value-pattern Construction

No observable difference exists between the mutant and original because the unused row doesn't participate in any calculations or output generation.

## Step 4 — Conclusion

The mutant is **equivalent** to the original. The mutation only allocates additional unused memory; all computations and outputs remain identical. to 0 and the rest will be zero-initialized (in C++, partial initializers zero the rest). No computation ever touches `accum[4][*]`, so the extra row remains completely unused throughout execution. The initialization behavior in C++ ensures all elements are properly zeroed regardless of array dimensions, making this mutant semantically identical to the original code.
